@UTF8
@Begin
@Languages:	eng
@Participants:	CHI Child, MOT Mother
@ID:	eng|corpus|CHI|3;00.||||Child|||
@ID:	eng|corpus|MOT|||||Mother|||
@Comment:	Prosodic features: lengthening (a:b), underline markers
@Comment:	Constructs: lengthening colons, underline_begin, underline_end
*CHI:	no:: I do:n't wa:nt to .
*MOT:	ple:ase eat your dinner .
*CHI:	we need  to do  something .
@Comment:	The pair below carries the SHAPE real CA data uses, which the two
	lines above do not: an underline begin glued INSIDE a word and an end
	glued to the START of the next. Attested in ca-data/Bergmann as
	`j:\x02\x01a \x02\x02da is noch mal s\x02\x01a\x02\x02:men`, written with escapes
	here so the COMMENT does not itself contain underline markers: it is
	parsed input, and a reflow that unbalanced them would present as a
	parser regression in the very check this fixture pins.
	The letters below are the
	fixture's own, the placement is the corpus's. Standalone markers alone
	never exercised the word-body scanner, which discarded these two for
	years behind a "skip for now".
*CHI:	we need to do something .
*MOT:	that is something else .
@End
